{{!-- The board itself: the lines between people, what each line is called, and the portraits.

      ITS OWN PARTIAL BECAUSE IT IS RE-RENDERED ON ITS OWN. Every live change to a map — somebody
      else moving a portrait, a link being drawn at the far end of the table — repaints THIS and
      swaps it into the open window, without re-rendering the Application. A full render would
      re-fit the board and throw away the corner the reader had zoomed into, which is exactly the
      state they were using when the change arrived. See RelationshipMapWindow#sync.

      Everything is positioned in PERCENTAGES of the board, which is a box of fixed notional size
      that utils/zoom-pan-surface.js scales and moves as a whole. So one set of numbers serves the
      stroke, the labels and the portraits at any magnification. --}}

{{!-- THE LINES. One stretched SVG for all of them, in the same 0-100 space everything else is
      placed in: preserveAspectRatio="none" maps that space onto the board whatever shape it is,
      and stretching a quadratic leaves a quadratic. `vector-effect` then keeps the stroke an even
      width in SCREEN space, which the stretch would otherwise smear along one axis.

      Takes no clicks: a drag that starts on empty board has to pan, and the line layer covers all
      of it. The label above each line is what a reader aims at. --}}
{{!-- THE FAMILY TREE, when that is what the reader has asked for, and empty every other time.

      A SECOND LAYER RATHER THAN MORE OF THE ONE BELOW, because these are not the same kind of
      mark. The lines below join two people and bow around whatever is in the way; each of these is
      one HOUSEHOLD drawn in one stroke: a bar between the parents, a stem down from it, a rail
      across the children and a drop into each of them. The square corners are the information, and
      they are what says "these four are siblings" where four separate curves would say only that
      four lines exist. Worked out in utils/relmap-tree.js.

      DECORATION, and deliberately: `aria-hidden`, no clicks, nothing to aim at. A stroke here
      stands for as many lines as the household has children, so there is no one line for a click
      to mean. A tie that needs fixing is fixed where it was written, on the ordinary board. --}}
<svg class="stonetop-relmap-tree" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none"
     aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  {{#each tree}}
  <path class="stonetop-relmap-tree-line{{#if couple}} is-couple{{/if}}" d="{{d}}"
        data-relmap-who="{{who}}" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></path>
  {{/each}}
</svg>

<svg class="stonetop-relmap-lines" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none"
     aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  {{#each edges}}
  <path class="stonetop-relmap-line stonetop-relmap-line--{{ink}}{{#if dotted}} is-dotted{{/if}}"
        data-relmap-line="{{id}}" d="{{d}}"
        data-relmap-who="{{a}} {{b}}"
        vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></path>
  {{!-- WHAT THE CLICK ACTUALLY LANDS ON: the same curve again, invisible, and wide.

        ⚠ THE PAINTED STROKE CANNOT TAKE THE CLICK ITSELF, and not for want of trying. It is three
        and a half units wide in a stretched 0-100 space with a non-scaling stroke — about four
        SCREEN pixels, and less than that is not a target, it is a dare. Widening the painted line
        to something aimable would be widening the drawing. So the target is its own stroke: the
        whole curve (never the broken one — the gap cut for a caption is the exact stretch a reader
        aims at), transparent, and wide enough to hit while talking.

        ⚠ AND IT IS THE ONE THING ON THIS LAYER THAT TAKES POINTER EVENTS. The layer covers the
        whole board and refuses them all, because a drag starting on empty board has to pan; these
        strokes opt back in from the stylesheet, which is the same arrangement the captions layer
        above uses, and for the same reason.

        NO ROLE AND NO TAB STOP. A line that says something is already reachable by keyboard — its
        caption is the button, and always has been. A line with nothing written on it is reachable
        from either of its two portraits, which are tab stops themselves. Eighty more stops between
        the bar and the board, most of them announced as an unnamed button, would cost every
        keyboard reader far more than it bought the one case. --}}
  <path class="stonetop-relmap-hit" data-relmap-hit="{{id}}" d="{{hit}}"
        aria-hidden="true" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></path>
  {{/each}}
</svg>

{{!-- WHICH WAY A LINK IS READ, where it is not simply mutual. Each head is its own fixed-size SVG
      rather than a marker on the path above, for the reason the journey's heads are: that path is
      stretched to the board, which would squash the triangle and (with orient="auto") aim it off
      any diagonal. These are turned by an angle already worked out in the board's real proportions,
      taken from the curve where the head actually sits rather than from the straight line between
      two portraits — so each lies along the line it belongs to even though that line is bowed.

      The triangle is `headD`, imported from utils/route-path.js rather than written out here: the
      expedition's two renderers draw the same shape from the same points, and a shape spelt out in
      three places is one that gets reshaped in two of them. --}}
{{#each heads}}
<svg class="stonetop-relmap-head stonetop-relmap-head--{{ink}}" viewBox="0 0 {{../headBox}} {{../headBox}}"
     aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" data-relmap-head="{{id}}" data-relmap-end="{{end}}"
     style="left:{{left}}%;top:{{top}}%;--relmap-turn:{{angle}}deg">
  <path d="{{../headD}}"></path>
</svg>
{{/each}}

{{!-- WHAT EACH LINK IS CALLED, SET IN THE LINE ITSELF.

      NOT THE STRETCHED viewBox ABOVE. The stroke layer maps 0-100 onto whatever shape the board
      happens to be, which shears every glyph along one axis; so the captions get a layer of their
      own, in board PIXELS and at the board's own proportions — one user unit is one board pixel and
      nothing is squashed.

      ⚠ STRAIGHT, AND TURNED TO THE ANGLE OF ITS LINE WHERE IT SITS. It was text on a PATH, which
      followed a bowed line glyph by glyph and was beautiful and unaffordable: warping gives every
      glyph its own transform, so the browser's glyph cache misses on every one, and with the halo
      stroked around each warped outline as well ONE TILE of this board cost 73ms to raster —
      panning it was a slideshow, and the whole application went with it, because Foundry's canvas
      shares that thread. The same words, straight, with the same halo: 1.2ms. Sixty times less.
      Most lines are near enough straight across the stretch one caption covers that the two are
      hard to tell apart; a strongly bowed line now carries its caption as a chord.

      ONE SVG FOR ALL OF THEM. Each caption used to carry its own small SVG over the patch of board
      its own words cover; a hundred separate roots is a hundred paint chunks, and it would put the
      lit-web dimming on a hundred elements rather than on one — opacity on a `<g>` costs an
      offscreen buffer apiece.

      THE LAYER IS NOT THE TARGET, THE WRITING IS. The layer covers the whole board, and a target
      that size would swallow every click meant for anything underneath it. So the layer takes no
      pointer events and the glyphs take them back (see the stylesheet), which makes the aimable
      area the words themselves — smaller than the chip it replaced, and exactly what the reader
      sees.

      THE WORDS ARE THE BUTTON, and carry a button's manners themselves: `role`, `tabindex` and the
      accessible name sit on the `<text>` because there is no HTML button around them any more — a
      wrapper would have to be either that enormous box or a second element kept in step with the
      glyphs, and both were what this layer was merged to be rid of. utils/relmap-drag.js gives
      them Enter and Space, which is the one thing a real button gave for free.

      A chip of paper rather than coloured text, still: the ink says which KIND of tie this is, and
      colour cannot be the only thing that says it — there is a reader at this table on a screen
      magnifier, and coloured small type is exactly what fails for them. So the line carries the
      colour (and, under the high-contrast skin, a dash pattern saying the same thing again with no
      colour at all), while the words stay body ink on paper at full contrast.

      THE TOOLTIP IS THE SENTENCE AND NOTHING ELSE, and the accessible name is not. What a tooltip
      is for here is the words the caption could not fit, so on a crowded board it is the only place
      the whole of one can be read; an instruction about clicking, repeated on eighty lines, buries
      that under something the reader learned the first time. A screen reader gets no other clue
      that this is a button, so the instruction stays in `aria-label`, which is why the two are
      separate fields and must not be re-merged.

      A POINT AND A TURN, in ABSOLUTE board pixels, which is exactly the space this one viewBox is
      in. The words are centred on that point and rotated about it (`text-anchor`,
      `dominant-baseline` and this `transform`), so a caption is placed the way an arrowhead is. --}}
<svg class="stonetop-relmap-labels" viewBox="0 0 {{boardWidth}} {{boardHeight}}" focusable="false">
  {{#each labels}}
  <g class="stonetop-relmap-label" data-relmap-edge="{{id}}" data-relmap-who="{{a}} {{b}}">
    <text class="stonetop-relmap-label-text" role="button" tabindex="0"
          data-relmap-words="{{id}}" data-tooltip="{{tooltip}}" aria-label="{{ariaLabel}}"
          x="{{x}}" y="{{y}}" transform="rotate({{angle}} {{x}} {{y}})">{{text}}</text>
  </g>
  {{/each}}
</svg>

{{!-- THE PEOPLE. A round portrait, a name under it, and a handle on the rim for drawing a line to
      somebody else.

      The face is a BUTTON and opens the actor's sheet; dragging the same element moves the portrait
      instead (utils/relmap-drag.js decides which happened, by how far the pointer travelled). The
      handle is separate so that "draw a line from here" is never something a reader does by
      accident on the way to reading a sheet.

      `imgStyle` is the crop the GM chose for this actor's portrait, from utils/portrait-frame.js —
      the same one the relationships table uses, so a face framed once is framed everywhere. --}}
{{!-- `is-centre` marks the one person a focus view is about. Nothing but the stylesheet reads it,
      and what it buys is a ring round the face in the middle: the lines already say which person
      the picture is around, but finding the middle of a star at four times magnification means
      panning until you meet it, and a mark you can recognise without reading a name saves that. --}}
{{!-- `is-own-ring` marks a face that arrived with a rim already painted on it — the playbook badge
      a character who never chose a portrait is given, each of them a woodcut inside a hand-drawn
      circle. The board's own rim then lands three pixels outside that one, and two concentric rings
      round a small picture read as a target rather than as somebody's face. The stylesheet drops
      ours on these and lets the drawn one be the rim. --}}
{{#each nodes}}
<div class="stonetop-relmap-node{{#if missing}} is-missing{{/if}}{{#if centre}} is-centre{{/if}}{{#if ownRing}} is-own-ring{{/if}}"
     data-relmap-node="{{id}}" style="left:{{left}}%;top:{{top}}%">
  <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-face" data-relmap-open="{{id}}"
          data-tooltip="{{tooltip}}" aria-label="{{tooltip}}">
    <span class="stonetop-relmap-portrait">
      {{#if img}}
      <img src="{{img}}" alt="" draggable="false" style="{{imgStyle}}">
      {{else}}
      <i class="fas fa-user"></i>
      {{/if}}
    </span>
  </button>
  <span class="stonetop-relmap-name">{{name}}</span>
  {{!-- Focusable, and it does something on a plain CLICK as well as on a drag: dragging it to
        another portrait draws the line, and clicking it asks who to draw to. Drag is the quick way
        and not the only way, which is what keeps the board usable from a keyboard and on a touch
        screen where a precise drag between two small circles is unpleasant. --}}
  {{!-- `canLink` and not `canEdit`: the family tree is still an editable board (a caption on it
        still opens its line) but drawing a NEW line onto it would put one where the tree cannot
        show it, so the handle is not offered there. --}}
  {{#if ../canLink}}
  <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-handle" data-relmap-handle="{{id}}"
          data-tooltip="{{../linkHint}}" aria-label="{{linkLabel}}">
    <i class="fas fa-link"></i>
  </button>
  {{/if}}
</div>
{{/each}}

{{!-- THE CAPTIONS ON A LIT WEB, drawn a second time.

      EMPTY UNTIL SOMEBODY RESTS ON A FACE, and filled by `_lightPerson` with a copy of each
      caption touching that person. The copies are what carries the highlight: full ink over the
      dimmed layer above, in a heavier weight, and — being last on the board — over the portraits
      too, which is the "brought forward" the old chip got from a `z-index`.

      ⚠ WHY A SECOND LAYER AND NOT A CLASS ON THE ORIGINAL. Dimming the quiet captions used to be
      an `opacity` on each of a hundred `<g>` elements, and opacity on a group costs an offscreen
      buffer apiece — a hundred of them, rebuilt every time the pointer crossed onto or off a
      portrait. One opacity on the layer as a whole costs one. So the layer is dimmed entire, and
      the handful of captions that should stay bright are drawn OVER it rather than exempted
      inside it.

      A COPY AND NOT A MOVE, so the layer above never changes and never loses its texture. The
      copy's rail is the ORIGINAL rail, referenced by the id it already has — which is also what
      makes a lit caption follow a live drag without anything here being told about it.

      DECORATION: `aria-hidden`, no clicks, nothing to aim at. The caption it copies is still in
      the layer above, still focusable, still says what it says. --}}
<svg class="stonetop-relmap-labels-lit" viewBox="0 0 {{boardWidth}} {{boardHeight}}"
     aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"></svg>
